# Line-ending discipline. The Twin's fingerprint normalizes to LF before
# hashing, so template identity survives any checkout convention — but the
# working tree should not manufacture avoidable diffs either, and a shell
# script with CRLF breaks bash outright. The listed classes normalize in
# the repository and shell scripts check out as LF; PowerShell keeps CRLF
# for notepad-friendliness on the operator machines that run it. No
# catch-all: a handful of V1 project files are stored with CRLF, and
# renormalizing them is churn this program has no reason to cause.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.sql text
*.fs text
*.fsx text
*.fsproj text
*.json text
*.md text
*.yml text
*.yaml text
*.mjs text
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
